            <ArticleTitle>Further evaluation of immunocytochemical staining in the diagnosis of early myocardial ischaemic/hypoxic damage.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>135-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The pattern of immunocytochemical staining with antibodies to caeruloplasmin, myosin, myoglobin and C-reactive protein seen in myocardium taken from deaths with macroscopic evidence of myocardial infarction and/or significant coronary artery atherosclerosis and from deaths with neither of these lesions has been correlated with H&amp;E, PTAH and HBFP staining of myocardium and circumstances of each death indicative of antemortem hypoxia and/or ischaemia. Loss of staining with these antibodies correlated well with fuchsinorrhagia and both techniques are more sensitive than H&amp;E and PTAH staining in the detection of early ischaemic/hypoxic damage to myocardium. However, their sensitivity is such that they appear to detect agonal changes and, therefore, cannot be used for specific diagnosis of early myocardial infarction.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
